% ============================================================================
% part-of-speech — an example word and which grammatical part of speech it
% is, in a sentence that shows it doing that job (adj-facts-stdlib,
% LANGUAGE).
% ============================================================================
% Every English word plays one of a small set of grammatical roles: a noun
% names a person, place, thing, or idea; a verb names an action or state; an
% adjective describes a noun. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? part_of_speech(queen, $Category)
%       % noun
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `part_of_speech(word, category)` carrying the source citation, so the
% lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the
% category WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains
% on any word the source does not classify this way. The query also runs
% BACKWARD — bind a category and recall which example word it names:
%
%     ? part_of_speech($Word, verb)
%       % run
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from Grammarly's "The 8 Parts of Speech"
% article, which gives one worked example sentence per part of speech
% covered here:
%
%     word (this table's atom label)   category    example sentence (verbatim)
%     --------------------------------  ----------  ---------------------------------------------
%     queen                             noun        "Cleopatra was the last queen of ancient Egypt."
%     run                                verb        "Run as fast as you can."
%     brown                             adjective   "I have a brown dog."
%
% WebFetch-verified TWICE before writing. `trust consensus` — Grammarly is a
% reputable, widely-cited grammar-content company, the same tier this
% stdlib already reserves for other non-.gov language sources (7ESL,
% Speakspeak) and the same source family already cited by
% `sentence-type.adj`. A word that is NOT one of these three — e.g.
% "slowly," a real word and an adverb, but not one of the three parts of
% speech this table covers — is deliberately NOT a row, so a recall
% ABSTAINS on it rather than inventing a classification the source never
% gave. (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table part_of_speech {
    columns word, category

    row (queen, noun)
    row (run, verb)
    row (brown, adjective)

    source "Cleopatra was the last queen of ancient Egypt."
    locator "https://www.grammarly.com/blog/parts-of-speech/the-8-parts-of-speech/"
    trust consensus
}
